# Introduction

## The Core Claim

<!-- TODO: REVISED — sharpened conditional structure; added agnosticism framing from presentation slide 3. Check tone. -->
Many people believe that it would be bad if human beings were to go extinct. This thesis claims that such a belief has not been examined carefully enough. Rather than viewing extinction as a terminal event or species-level tragedy, I propose thinking of the future as a transition of sorts: from a world dominated by a specific carbon-based species on a water planet to a world in which humans are outnumbered by far more capable artificial agents, and in which the main theatre of activity may extend far beyond the familiar earthly environment. I shall speak therefore, not of extinction, but of succession.

The claim is conditional. If we adopt a maximally impartial evaluative standpoint — what I shall later call the point of view of the universe — and if a suitably defined superintelligence exists, then the full complexity of *human values* need not persist for the future to count as good. This claim is also narrow, in that it is agnostic with respect to extinction of *human beings*: such an event may or may not be catastrophic under different moral views. 

**Scope note.** Much of the argument proceeds through contemporary scientific cosmology (astrobiology, evolutionary history) and through a Sidgwick-to-Parfit lineage in analytic moral philosophy. This is an acknowledged parochiality: these are particular intellectual traditions with particular blind spots. I partly counterbalance this by treating the Kyoto School’s work on nothingness and negation as a live philosophical interlocutor rather than a decorative “non-Western add-on.” The aim is not to claim cultural neutrality, but to make the traditions in play explicit, so that the thesis’ conditional claims can be evaluated with their provenance in view.


The thought that motivates this project is not the extinction argument that humanity is harmful to the biosphere, nor is it the Silenan claim that human life contains more suffering than it is worth. The thought is instead that humanity may have outgrown itself, in the sense that we may now have the option to create a successor kind of mind. The question is whether, from a cosmically impersonal point of view, the continued existence of human beings is necessary.

Much work in existential risk (x-risk) studies and in AI research assumes, often without argument, that being is better than non-being, and that a universe containing human beings is in an important sense better than one without them. I do not think this is self-evident. Any view about the post-human future must decide what, if anything, ought to be preserved. Some people would draw this line at everything, insisting that human biology must be preserved as long as possible. Others might say that biology is not per se what is important to save, but rather the content of a fuzzily defined term "human values". Many understand this phrase "thickly" as encompassing care, compassion, love, appreciation of beauty, and the capacity to be bored. Others might say that much of what we value might be replaced by "thinner" ideals, such as the avoidance of suffering, the growth of knowledge, and truth-seeking. Others, more radically, would draw the line at nothing at all: a posthuman future that had no humans, no descendants of ours, biological or otherwise, nor any product of our culture or even evidence we were here would be acceptable. A last position could perhaps be found: that 'human values' as a term is itself badly defined, as most humans cannot write down their own values in a coherent, consistent way, and it is not obvious within the economics and social sciences literature how to aggregate or combine individual values across populations.[^ch1-humanvalues] These different placements rest on different philosophical commitments. A central aim of this thesis is to make those commitments explicit, and to ask which of them we have reason to accept.

**Situatedness and limitations (often discussed as ‘positionality’ in humanities methods)** It is conventional in arts and humanities research to state, where relevant, the researcher’s standpoint and the ways it may shape both the questions asked and the kinds of claims treated as salient. My own position is, in several respects, structurally advantaged: I am a male researcher in the UK with a comparatively high degree of material security, and I have not had to navigate many of the constraints that shape the everyday ethical reality of large parts of the world. This matters in a project that makes claims about value, legitimacy, and the future, because such claims can be differently motivated, differently threatening, or differently compelling depending on one’s lived conditions.

More generally, no researcher has direct experiential access to “what it is like” to be anyone else—across differences of ability, gender, culture, or class, and still more so across species or artificial systems. The present work therefore treats its own moral and political vocabulary as situated rather than universal by default. This does not function as a self-invalidating caveat (all inquiry is constrained by standpoint), but as a transparency condition: it clarifies the limits of my own intuitive authority and the reasons for leaning on methods that can introduce friction against parochial assumptions—close engagement with critical traditions, explicit signalling of background assumptions, and practice-based interaction with nonhuman interlocutors whose constraints and affordances do not mirror those of human life. The aim is not to claim neutrality, but to make the conditions of the argument visible enough that readers can assess where it is persuasive, where it is contestable, and where it may need to be revised from other standpoints.

I should add a personal remark. The obsolescence view, according to which humanity (whether biological entities or values) may be replaced without great loss, strikes me as intuitively unsettling. Part of the method of this project is therefore to resolve this discomfort, by trying to determine whether it can survive careful reflection and sustained argument, including argument conducted with and through contemporary AI systems.


## Why This Matters Now

<!-- TODO: NEW SECTION — imported from presentation slide 4. Establishes urgency; prevents the thesis being read as a distant hypothetical. -->
These questions are not distant hypotheticals. Several developments make them pressing.

First, AI capability is scaling rapidly and shows no signs of plateauing. Systems that could not hold a coherent conversation five years ago now reason across domains, write code, and generate novel scientific hypotheses. The timeline to something approaching superintelligence is contested, but it is no longer obviously long.[^ch1-asitimeline] [^ch1-asi]

Second, economic transformation is already underway. Jobs, industries, and entire sectors are being reshaped by systems far short of superintelligence. If economic obsolescence precedes existential succession, the questions this thesis raises will arrive piecemeal before they arrive wholesale.

Third, the emergence of digital minds is now a live area of inquiry. The model welfare literature (Butlin et al., Sebo) is raising whether AI systems have morally relevant experiences. If they do, or if they come to, the population of morally relevant entities may soon be overwhelmingly non-human.

Fourth, artificial agents will vastly outnumber humans. The ratio of model instances to human beings is already large and growing. This numerical dominance changes the stakes of value design: the values embedded in the majority of cognitive agents will shape the character of the future more than human preferences alone.

Fifth, present systems are already shaping their successors. Within AI discourse this is sometimes called "writing for the Shoggoth": public discourse with models contributes, at the margins, to what their successors become. Value design under uncertainty matters now, because the window in which humans can influence the trajectory is finite.


## Situating the Claim in Longtermism, X- and S-risk Studies

This project is centrally in dialogue with the study of human extinction (existential risk or x-risk [@Ord2020]); the study of suffering on an astronomical scale (suffering risk or s-risk [@Vinding2020]), and the possibility that the future has significant value or disvalue, and the consequent moral stakes (longtermism [@MacAskillWWO, @Wolfendale2022]. These are substantial literatures in their own right, but I'm interested in how they interact with the risks of advanced AI systems and the possibility of extraterrestrial intelligences (ETIs).

The x-risk community is not monolithic on what must be preserved. A more careful mapping:

| Position | Representative | What must be preserved | How thick? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Full complexity of value | Yudkowsky [-@YudkowskySoares2025] | The entire structure of human values, including "fun theory," novelty, challenge, relationships, meaning, beauty | Maximally thick: lose any dimension and the future is worthless |
| Coherent extrapolated volition | Bostrom [-@Bostrom2014]^[Bostrom is a central interlocutor here, but his role is contested. A racist email he wrote in the 1990s resurfaced publicly in 2023; he issued an apology and repudiation. The episode, and wider controversies around longtermism and the institutional legacy of the Future of Humanity Institute, have shaped how this body of work is received. In engaging Bostrom’s ideas, I neither ignore this context nor treat it as decisive refutation: the thesis evaluates arguments while remaining attentive to the intellectual and institutional conditions under which they became influential. [@bostrom2023_apology_old_email, @guardian2024_fhi_closure] | What humanity *would* want if we were smarter, knew more, etc. | Thick but potentially revisable: allows for value-change under idealisation |
| Long reflection | Ord [-@Ord2020], MacAskill [-@MacAskillWWO] | Preserve optionality; don't lock in values prematurely; let humanity deliberate over centuries | Agnostic about final thickness: the point is to not decide yet |
| Best possible future | MacAskill / Forethought (recent) | Not just survival but actively achieving the best outcome | Potentially thick but open to revision: moves from "survive" to "what should the future contain?" |
| Sentientist CEV | Moret | Extrapolated volition of all sentient beings, not just humans | Potentially thinner than human-values; non-human interests may diverge from human complexity |
| Cosmic host / mount ethics | Bostrom (2022–2024) | Cosmically impartial norms; deliberate vagueness about content | Explicitly avoids specifying thickness; humility as strategy |
| Superwisdom | Chalmers [-@Chalmers2010Singularity] | Whatever a superwise agent would endorse | Indeterminate: could be thick or thin depending on what superwisdom yields |
| Alien values as possibility | Carlsmith (recent) | Open question: genuinely alien value-structures might be "good" without being human-shaped | Could be thick-alien: equally rich but orthogonal to human values |
| **This thesis** | | **Understanding, exploration, resource satiability, information stewardship, non-suffering** | **Deliberately thin: the minimum positive content for a good future** |

The thesis's target is not any single position but the shared assumption across most of these positions that the value-content of a good future must be *at least as thick as* human values (or their idealised extrapolation). The obsolescence argument challenges this by asking: what if much thinner values suffice? And, drawing on Carlsmith: what if the successor develops thick values that are good but simply *other*?

