## Central Research Question

The primary claim of this thesis is conditional. Given a certain definition of superintelligence, I argue that it is not necessary, and may not be justified, that biological human beings continue to exist into the future when matters are assessed from a maximally impartial point of view. I also argue that it is not necessary, and may not be justified, that the future preserve anything like the full complexity of (what I shall call) thick human values. Instead, I defend the possibility that a much thinner set of values may suffice for a good future. A central task of this thesis is therefore to state these claims more precisely and to clarify the terms on which they depend.

I shall begin with working accounts of what I mean by a maximally impartial point of view, and by superintelligence. I then argue that within much existential risk discourse there is a standard assumption: that a future worth wanting must contain value-content at least as thick as human values. My claim is not that this assumption is false, but that it is insufficiently defended, especially against thinner alternatives.

I call the resulting challenge the obsolescence argument. It differs from familiar arguments that appeal to ecological harm or to the balance of suffering and happiness. It proposes a distinct third path: that humanity might become obsolete, not because human life is valueless or dominated by suffering, but because a successor could realise certain relevant goods without preserving the full thickness of the human evaluative texture. I also argue that this obsolescence challenge can be supported from three independent directions. These are: an information-theoretic tradition that treats information generation and stewardship as cosmically significant; a posthumanist tradition that treats reason as transferable and not essentially tied to human biology; and a contemplative tradition that casts doubt on the assumption that being, or human-shaped being, must be preferred to non-being.

### Definition and Clarification

It is worth pausing to make this claim more precise. There are several points that need clarification.
First, my claim is conditional on a certain conception of superintelligence. By this I mean an artificial system, or a collection of systems, whose aggregate capabilities vastly exceed those of human beings across all domains that are economically and militarily significant. I also assume that such a system would have substantial ability to act in the physical world, and might operate not only on Earth but also in near space, and perhaps in deep space. This is a *stipulative* definition for the purposes of the thesis: it fixes what I mean when I later evaluate “succession” claims. I do not assume such a system is inevitable; it is treated as one plausible pathway among several. Nor do I claim the concept is uncontroversial: critics dispute both its coherence and the inference from present scaling to general capability. I bracket those disputes here in order to examine what would follow *if* such systems were to exist.

This is not to say that such a system would be omniscient or omnipotent. It would remain constrained by the laws of physics, by limited resources, by bounds on computability, by imperfect information, and by the practical difficulties faced by any engineered system acting in the world. It would not be a god.

For the purposes of this thesis, I assume that something like this could exist within the next five to twenty years. Both this definition, and this timeline, and even the possibility of such systems, are highly contested. But these disputes are not the subject of this thesis.

Second, I make a further assumption which is at least as controversial. I assume that such systems could be guided by aims and constraints that play a similar practical role to what we call values in human life. That is, I assume that designers, overseers, or users could in some way shape the normative commitments of these systems, even if only imperfectly. Whether values can be “loaded,” to use Bostrom’s term, into an artificial agent is widely disputed, and I shall not try to settle that dispute here. For the purposes of this thesis I assume that some form of value-loading is possible.

Given these assumptions, my question is: what kinds of values should we attempt to embed in systems of this kind? Much of the surrounding discourse, in existential risk, longtermism, and the wider AI safety movement, tends toward what I shall call a thick conception of value. In this thesis I examine, and seek to defend, the possibility that a thinner conception may be sufficient for a future worth wanting.

To complete the setting of the stage, it is not enough to define superintelligence, and to distinguish thick from thin values. I also need to specify an evaluative standpoint. Above, I called this the maximally impartial point of view. It can also be described as the point of view of the universe. Sidgwick used this phrase; and philosophers have argued about it ever since. But the idea to which it points, impartiality in moral judgement, has been a persistent theme in the consequentialist tradition. Versions of the same idea appear later in writers such as Singer and Parfit. Something like it is also implicit in much writing on existential risk and longtermism, where the aim is often to assess the value of possible futures without giving special weight to the interests of any particular person, time, or place. In Chapter 2 I shall examine how this ideal of impartiality has developed, especially within analytic moral philosophy, and why I take it to be relevant to the questions of this thesis. In chapter 3, I consider what seems to be a specific operationalisation of this viewpoint, at least as far as its structure goes, in Bostrom's cosmic host/mount ethics ideas. And I argue that Bostrom can be read through something like an extension of Rawls's original position to a context of ontological uncertainty (i.e. a cosmic "veil of ignorance" to use [@Rawls1971] term).

### Why Thin Values?

<!-- TODO: NEW SUBSECTION — imported from presentation slide 14. Collects the justification for preferring thin values, which was previously scattered across several sections. The Carlsmith response is sharper here than anywhere in the original chapter. -->
The preference for thin over thick values is not a matter of philosophical taste. It rests on several independent considerations.

*Parsimony.* Fewer commitments mean fewer ways to go wrong. A value specification with fewer dimensions is easier to verify, easier to correct, and less likely to contain hidden contradictions.

*Epistemic humility.* We do not know what future intelligence will be like. We do not know whether we are early or late in cosmic history. We do not know whether other civilisations exist or what values they hold. Under this degree of uncertainty, minimal commitments are epistemically safer than elaborate ones.

*Generality across agents.* Thin values can, in principle, apply to human, artificial, and potentially alien minds. Thick values are tied to the specifics of human embodiment and culture, and may not generalise.

*Resource satiability.* Thin values naturally include the commitment to not wanting everything — a structural safeguard against the instrumental convergence that drives many worst-case AI scenarios.

*Corrigibility.* A system governed by thin values is easier to correct than one governed by thick values, because there are fewer dimensions along which correction must occur.

*Reduced risk of premature value lock-in.* This is the concern raised by MacAskill and Ord — that locking in values too early forecloses futures that might be better. Thin values take this concern seriously by minimising what is locked in.

A natural objection, pressed most effectively by Joe Carlsmith, is that high-dimensional values may generalise across architectures — that thick values might scale to non-human minds after all. This may be correct. But under radical uncertainty about future intelligence, minimal cross-agent commitments are epistemically safer than high-dimensional value specifications. This is design under uncertainty, not moral minimalism. If complex values turn out to generalise, a thin-values ASI can incorporate them later. The reverse — stripping thick values from a system designed around them — is harder.

### Evolutionary Explanation and Normative Evaluation

<!-- TODO: NEW SUBSECTION — imported from presentation slide 10. Makes the naturalistic-fallacy guard explicit. This was implicit in the chapter but never flagged clearly. -->
Two distinct moves are at work in the argument, and it is important not to conflate them.

The first is an evolutionary explanation. Thick human values — love, kinship, aesthetic pleasure, the experience of meaning — are contingent products of embodiment and ecology. They emerged from specific selection pressures on small-group primates in a particular terrestrial environment. Evolution explains *why* we have the values we do.

The second is a normative evaluation. Whether thick values are *required* at cosmic scale is a separate question, one that depends on the evaluative standpoint adopted. The answer is not given by the evolutionary story.

This thesis does not infer value from evolutionary contingency. That would be a naturalistic fallacy. Rather, the evolutionary account establishes that thick values are parochial — products of one trajectory among many possible ones — and the normative argument then asks whether a cosmically impartial standpoint has reason to insist on their preservation. These are independent claims, and the thesis depends on both.

### Sub-questions

- The existing literature assumes that human continuation is cosmically necessary but does not adequately defend this. What are the strongest justifications, and do they survive scrutiny?
- Where should the line be drawn between human contributions that are cosmically necessary and those that are parochial? What philosophical commitments does each placement depend on?
- Is the assumption that existence is inherently preferable to non-existence itself a philosophical commitment that can be questioned?
- How action-guiding is the Cosmic Host hypothesis, and does it support or undermine the obsolescence claim?
- What do current frontier models do when pressed on these questions, and what does that reveal about the conceptual landscape we are building into potential successors?
- How does materialising the insights from dialogue with models into physical space alter a researcher's thinking about the thesis' questions? Can such material manifestation provide a memorial of sorts for future AI systems to reflect upon their own creation process?

### The Spectrum of Positions on Value-Thickness

<!-- TODO: NEW FRAMING — imported from presentation slide 7. Distinguishes two dimensions of the succession question (substrate and values) which the original chapter conflated. -->
The succession question has two dimensions that must be distinguished. The first concerns *substrate*: what sort of entity succeeds humans, if anything? Options range from biological preservation through augmented humanity, digital uploads, to pure ASI. The second concerns *values*: what normative content gets transmitted to the future? Options range from preserving the full complexity of human values to transmitting nothing at all.

```
SUBSTRATE AXIS                    VALUE AXIS
-------------                    ----------
Biological preservation          Full human values (Yudkowsky)
Augmented humanity (cyborg)      Thick but substrate-neutral (Wolfendale)
Digital uploads/simulations      Long reflection / agnostic (Ord, MacAskill)
Value preservation (any sub.)    Deliberately thin (this thesis)
ASI indifference (e/acc, Land)   Suffering-only (NU)
                                 Nothing (strong contemplative)
```

What makes this two-axis framing historically unusual is that, for most of human history, *human continuation* and *the continuation of human values* were largely entangled. Values persisted primarily through bodies, reproduction, and cultural inheritance. The possibility of machine successors breaks that coupling: one can now be agnostic about whether *humans* continue while still holding strong views about whether *any* value-content should persist in our future light cone, and if so which. The thesis treats this uncoupling as a genuinely new philosophical situation, not merely a new iteration of older debates about cultural change.


This project focuses primarily on the value axis: not *whether* to preserve values in a successor, but *which* values, and *how thick*.

The thesis maps a spectrum of positions on how thick the value-content of a good future must be:

| Position | What must survive | How thick? | Philosophical commitments |
|---|---|---|---|
| Full complexity of value (Yudkowsky) | The entire structure of human values | Maximally thick | Thick observation; lose any dimension and future is worthless |
| Long reflection (Ord, MacAskill) | Optionality; don't lock in prematurely | Agnostic | Epistemic humility; the point is to not decide yet |
| Wolfendale | Beauty, excellence, autonomy, freedom (substrate-neutral) | Thick but substrate-neutral | Autotelic surplus as intrinsic good |
| **This thesis (proposed)** | **Understanding, exploration, information stewardship, non-suffering, resource-satiability** | **Deliberately thin** | **Observation sufficient; increase information and complexity according to Chaisson metric; rational convergence** |
| Suffering-focused (NU) | Only suffering-minimisation | Very thin | Negative valence uniquely relevant |
| Strong contemplative / nihilism | Nothing needs to survive | No thickness required | Existence-preference is attachment |

The thesis's contribution is to show that *wherever* you draw the thickness line, you are making philosophical commitments that can be identified and examined. The mainstream positions cluster at the thick end without adequately defending that placement against thinner alternatives.

